\paragraph{Three levels of RL on the flow model.}
Table~\ref{tab:main} reads as three levels. \emph{Score-function (GRPO).} It works: every task improves at the deployment sampler, by $+3.5$ and $+4.1$ on Sudoku and K\&K, where the base already solves two thirds of the problems, but by only $+0.9$ on Countdown and $+2.5$ on Game24, the tasks with the most headroom. Its weaknesses are the ones \S\ref{sec:grpo} predicts for a noisy estimator: the gains are smallest where the headroom is largest, and where it does move the policy it is seed-sensitive (per-seed peaks spread over $6.6$ points on Sudoku, one seed never rising above the base). \emph{Pathwise with a scalar critic.} Under the same algorithm the pooled critic is already the stronger estimator where the headroom is large, $+6.0$ on Countdown, $+10.1$ on K\&K and $+6.4$ on Game24 over the base, but it is the less reliable one: on Sudoku it never clears the base ($66.3$) and every seed collapses to $62$--$64$ by 1000 iterations. \emph{Pathwise with the 2D critic.} Replacing the pooled value by the per-position field changes nothing else in the algorithm and adds $+2.0$, $+4.7$, $+2.0$ and $+7.4$ points on the four tasks; per-seed peaks agree within $0.3$--$1.1$ points on every task and no run collapses within its schedule (on Sudoku the peak comes at 500 iterations and the runs then drift to $64$--$66$). It leads GRPO on every task, by $+7.1$ on Countdown, $+1.3$ on Sudoku, $+8.0$ on K\&K and $+11.3$ on Game24, on every seed except one Sudoku seed of GRPO. The ODE sampler (Table~\ref{tab:ode}) orders the levels the same way, with the 2D critic first in every column. The estimator matters where there is headroom, and the field is what makes it reliable.

\paragraph{The AR control.}
The score-function estimator is not weak in itself. A GPT-2 fine-tuned on the same data climbs from 44.2 to 53.9 on Countdown and from 26.9 to 32.2 on Game24 under token-level GRPO/PPO with textbook clip statistics, larger gains than any score-function variant achieves on the flow model (Table~\ref{tab:sf_variants}); on K\&K, where its supervised model already solves 92.9\%, and on Sudoku, where it solves 1\%, the same recipe leaves it where it started. Each architecture improves through the gradient channel its action space admits: the flow model gains $+13.8$ on Game24 through its native estimator. d1 \citep{zhao2025d1}, the standard recipe for masked diffusion models, run unchanged on our tasks and data, adds $1.5$--$3.4$ points to its supervised model on Countdown, K\&K and Game24 and never produces a solved Sudoku grid (Appendix~\ref{app:d1}).

\paragraph{The loop and its controls.}
The loop starts from the best 2D-critic seed of each task; the controls run the identical rounds from the SFT base, from the best GRPO seed, and from the 2D policy with the gold labels of the same pool problems in place of its own samples. On Countdown the loop is where the 2D policy pays off. Two plain rounds pull the deployment sampler down ($50.4\!\to\!47.9\!\to\!46.4$) while the ODE sampler gains eight points and the pool's coverage shrinks; rounds 3--4, whose pool is sampled with best-of-4 pruning and guidance by the jointly trained 2D critic, restore coverage and lift the deployment sampler to $\OURScd$ (Appendix~\ref{app:cd_loop}), against $46.2$ from the base, $45.1$ from the GRPO policy and $42.6$ with gold labels. On Game24 four rounds take the ODE-64 sampler from $42.2$ to $50.5$ while the deployment sampler gives back three points ($59.3\!\to\!56.0$; Figure~\ref{fig:curves}, right): the loop transfers what the policy can do to the cheap sampler but does not raise a policy already at the ceiling of the split; the same rounds from the base, from the GRPO policy and on gold labels end at $46.1$, $50.5$ and $49.2$. On K\&K five rounds on fresh problems compound from $80.0$ to $\OURSkk$ ($83.2$, $90.2$, $94.3$, $\OURSkkfour$, $\OURSkk$; ODE-64 from $70.4$ to $\OURSkko$); the loop compounds from the SFT base and from the GRPO policy as well ($99.6$ and $98.3$ after five rounds, against $99.7$ with gold labels), so on K\&K the rounds, not the RL level, close the task: the loop is bounded by coverage, and any policy that solves a third of the pool climbs the same ladder (Appendix~\ref{app:kk_by_n}).

\paragraph{Where it does not help.}
Sudoku's loop is null from every start ($71.3\!\to\!71.4$ on 50k fresh puzzles from the 2D policy; $65.5$ from the base, $71.9$ from the GRPO policy's $72.8$, $72.2$ with gold labels): solutions are unique, so a policy only re-solves what it already solves, and the cheap samplers already sit within five points of the deployment one.

